 listened to the wind, the leaves, the distant river, and the silence between them. He said the hours were not numbers but breaths, and that time was a river that flowed through all things.

One day a young traveler arrived, carrying a pocket watch that ticked with perfect regularity. The traveler said, “Time is a machine. It is measured, divided, and controlled.” The old clockmaker smiled and replied, “Time is a story. It is remembered, felt, and lived.”

The traveler did not understand. He wanted to know how many seconds were in an hour, how many hours in a day, how many days in a year. The old clockmaker answered, “How many breaths have you taken today? How many moments have you truly lived?”

The traveler frowned. “That is not how time works,” he said. “Time is objective. It is the same for everyone.”

The old clockmaker shook his head. “Time is subjective. It is different for everyone. When you are happy, it flies. When you are sad, it drags. When you are in love, it stops. When you are waiting, it stretches. Time is not a machine. It is a feeling.”

